            {
              "type": "reasoning.summary",
              "summary": "**Evaluating confidence generation**\n\nI'm considering how counts matter but don't really lead to defined confidence generation mathematically. It seems like peer answers could update overall demand, but those are just recommendations and don’t guarantee anything. I wonder if I should combine surveys and peer reports for better accuracy. The need to only count each observation once is crucial, but the peer reports seem indirect. So, I need to review all reports and see how the hidden counts fit together.**Analyzing confidence generation**\n\nI'm thinking about how confidence should derive from actual posterior values for each analyst. The value I have, m3/m2 .685715, seems invalid, which suggests that the reported confidence might not be exactly usable. I wonder if that number reflects confidence after mixing several inputs? If analysts have multiple surveys and peer reports, it makes sense the confidence level could vary. I should update based on peer answers, calculating prior odds and probabilities more accurately to refine the overall confidence.**Considering peer confidence**\n\nI'm thinking that peer confidence could actually provide useful information. For m1, if A has high confidence, it identifies 0 high, assuming that it's calibrated correctly. I wonder how much we can rely on those calibrations to interpret the data accurately. It seems important to make sure that these confidence levels are properly aligned with the observations to draw valid conclusions. I'll need to keep this in mind when evaluating the overall data set.",
